title: The Piedmont Klan Crisis
subtitle: A governor defies the will of the courts.
tags: event
new-page: true
view-if: NC_gub_winner = "rep" and NC_kkk_strength + NC_whiteL_strength >= 80
max-visits: 1
on-arrival: {!
    Q.NC_kkk_strength = (Q.NC_kkk_strength || 0) + 4;
    Q.kkk_strength += 1;
    Q.NC_freemen_rep -= 3;
    Q.NC_rural_dem += 1;
!}

= The Piedmont Klan Crisis

Reports from North Carolina's Piedmont describe a campaign of murder and intimidation against <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican**</span> officials and their supporters. In one county, a masked band seized a Black town commissioner from his home and hanged him in the courthouse square. A warning left on the body threatened Black and white <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republicans**</span> alike.

Several weeks later, a <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican**</span> state senator was lured into a courthouse basement during a <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democratic**</span> nominating convention and stabbed to death. The coroner's inquest returned a finding of death at the hands of persons unknown. The local sheriff, himself suspected of Klan membership, helped conduct the inquiry.

The governor has repeatedly petitioned for authority to suppress the violence. The legislature has now granted him additional militia powers, though it struck out a provision permitting the suspension of habeas corpus. Washington has issued no orders for military deployment against the Klan.

The governor has acted without waiting for further instructions. He has raised a militia in the western mountains, where Unionist sentiment remains strongest, and placed a former Union raider in command. Within days, the force occupied two county seats and arrested a number of suspected Klansmen, including a former congressman and both county sheriffs. Klan activity in the affected counties has since fallen sharply.

The violent crackdown have provoked a constitutional dispute as the state's chief justice has issued a writ of habeas corpus demanding that the prisoners be brought before the court. The governor has refused to comply, despite lacking statutory authority to suspend the writ. He has now appealed to Washington for support.

[? if NC_occupation_posture >= 1 : An estimated [+ NC_garrison_troops +] thousand federal troops are stationed in North Carolina. Their presence may prove decisive if the dispute escalates further. ?][? if NC_occupation_posture < 1 : No federal troops are stationed in North Carolina, leaving the state government to confront the crisis on its own. ?]

- @back_governor: Back the governor and permit the military tribunal to proceed
- @honor_writ: Order the governor to honor the writ and submit the prisoners to the courts

@back_governor
on-arrival: {!
    let troops = Q.NC_garrison_troops || 0;
    Q.NC_kkk_strength = Math.max(0, (Q.NC_kkk_strength || 0) - Math.min(28, 14 + troops * 0.8));
    Q.NC_freemen_rep += 3;
    Q.NC_rural_dem += 3;
    Q.NC_middle_class_dem += 2;
    Q.radical_strength += 2;
    Q.liberal_dissent += 4;
    Q.conservative_dissent += 2;
    Q.dem_relation -= 5;
!}
go-to: fallout_defied

Washington has endorsed the governor's actions. The military tribunal retains custody of the prisoners, and Klan operations in the two counties have largely ceased. The decision has drawn immediate criticism from <span style="color: #DAA520;">**Liberal Republicans**</span>, who warn that the administration has sanctioned imprisonment without trial. <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democratic**</span> newspapers describe the affair as an assault on constitutional government.

@honor_writ
on-arrival: {!
    Q.NC_kkk_strength = Math.max(0, (Q.NC_kkk_strength || 0) - 6);
    Q.NC_freemen_rep -= 2;
    Q.NC_rural_dem += 1;
    Q.liberal_dissent = Math.max(0, (Q.liberal_dissent || 0) - 2);
    Q.dem_relation += 2;
!}
go-to: fallout_lawful

The administration has instructed the governor to comply with the writ. The prisoners are brought before the courts, but the prosecutions quickly falter. Witnesses refuse to testify in the presence of suspected Klansmen, and local juries decline to convict their neighbors. Within weeks, the prisoners have been released.

Klan activity resumes in the surrounding counties. Critics of the decision argue that the courts have proved unable to protect those who testify against the organization.

@fallout_defied
go-to: impeachment

The controversy carries into the state legislative elections. The governor's militia maintains a presence at polling places in several counties, and the election passes without major violence. Nevertheless, the <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democrats**</span> make the governor's actions their principal campaign issue, accusing him of using military force to intimidate the electorate. They win control of the legislature.

Reports that prisoners were permitted to cast ballots from the courthouse jail provide further ammunition to the opposition. <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democratic**</span> newspapers describe the affair as the governor's war, a phrase that soon spreads beyond North Carolina.

@fallout_lawful
go-to: impeachment

The governor complies with the court's order, but the damage to his political standing has already been done. The militia's presence at polling places remains a central issue in the legislative campaign, and the <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democrats**</span> win control of the legislature.

The governor's supporters argue that he acted against a genuine campaign of political murder. His opponents answer that he exceeded his lawful authority. Klan activity has subsided in two counties but continues elsewhere in the state.

<span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democratic**</span> newspapers continue to call the affair the governor's war, and the phrase becomes a familiar refrain in the new legislature.

@impeachment

The new legislature has voted to impeach the governor. The articles of impeachment accuse him of fabricating the extent of the insurrection, imprisoning citizens without lawful authority and misusing public funds.

The trial continues for several weeks. Prosecutors present testimony concerning the militia's arrests and alleged abuses. The defense calls witnesses who describe Klan murders, threats and the failure of local authorities to intervene. Both sides produce substantial evidence, but the outcome increasingly appears to depend on the political balance in the Senate rather than the testimony alone.

- @defend_governor: Rally the party behind the governor
- @let_him_fall: Remain neutral and allow the legislature to decide his fate

@defend_governor
on-arrival: {!
    Q.radical_strength += 3;
    Q.freemen_rep += 2;
    Q.NC_freemen_rep += 3;
    Q.NC_rural_dem += 2;
    Q.liberal_dissent += 2;
    Q.PoliticalCostPenalty += 2;
    Q.dem_relation -= 3;
!}

We commit our remaining political capital to the governor's defense, but the effort fails. The Senate convicts him on most of the charges and removes him from office.

Black legislators warn that his removal will leave <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican**</span> officials and their constituents exposed to renewed intimidation. The governor is in Washington seeking an audience with the President when the verdict is delivered.

@let_him_fall
on-arrival: {!
    Q.moderate_strength += 2;
    Q.liberal_dissent = Math.max(0, (Q.liberal_dissent || 0) - 3);
    Q.radical_dissent += 4;
    Q.NC_freemen_rep -= 4;
    Q.NC_middle_class_dem += 2;
    Q.dem_relation += 3;
!}

We decline to intervene. The Senate convicts the governor on most of the charges and removes him from office. <span style="color: #9D2933;">**Moderates**</span> welcome the verdict as a restoration of constitutional order. <span style="color: #7A1F5C;">**Radicals**</span> accuse the administration of abandoning the one Southern governor willing to confront the Klan by force.

<span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican**</span> officials across the South follow the proceedings closely. The verdict sends a warning to governors who might consider using extraordinary powers against organized political violence, particularly when their opponents control the legislature.